          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em closed monoidal category
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi 𝟙
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \big( \mathcal{C}, \, \mathbb{1},\, \otimes \big)
            that is also a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+category"} closed category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ∈
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \,\in\, \mathcal{C}
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi Y
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi C
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      (-) \otimes Y  \;\colon\; C \longrightarrow C
              %p
                of forming the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
                with
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %li
              %p
                has a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi Y
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi C
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      [Y,-] \;\colon\; C \longrightarrow C
              %p
                forming the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal-hom"} internal-hom
                out of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
          %p
            in that for all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triples"} triples
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X,\, Y,\, Z
            there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphism"} natural
            %a{:href => "adjoint+functor#InTermsOfHomIsomorphism"} hom-isomorphism
            of the following form:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≃
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Hom_{\mathcal{C}}(X \otimes Y,\, Z)
                  \\;\simeq\;
                  Hom_{\mathcal{C}}\big(X, [Y,Z] \big)
                  \\,.
          %p
            The archetypical example is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed category
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sets"} Sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
            is given by forming
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+sets"} function sets
            %a{:href => "adjoint+functor#InTermsOfHomIsomorphism"} hom-isomorphism
            expresses the fact that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x,y) \mapsto f(x,y)
            of two variables is equivalently a function of a single variable with values in functions of the other variable:
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ↦
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \mapsto \big(y \mapsto f(x,y) \big)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+logic"} formal logic
            this is known as “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/currying"}> currying
            ”.
          %p
            More abstractly, from the point of view of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            theory, closed monoidal categories may be regarded as a special case of the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+pseudomonoids"} closed pseudomonoids
            in a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+bicategory"} monoidal bicategory
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %h3#symmetric_closed_monoidal_category Symmetric closed monoidal category
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is
            %strong closed
            (cf.
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+closed+category"} symmetric monoidal closed category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in C_0
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} - \otimes b \colon C \to C
            has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi b
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [b,-] \colon C \to C
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo ∀
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi C
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi C
                  %munderover
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ⊥
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %munder
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi b
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟵
                      %mrow
                        %mi b
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi C
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\underset{b \in C}{\forall}
                  \\;\;
                  C
                  \\underoverset
                  {\underset{[b,-]}{\longrightarrow}}
                  {\overset{b \otimes (-)}{\longleftarrow}}
                  {\;\;\;\;\;\bot\;\;\;\;\;}
                  C
                  \\,.
          %p
            This
            %a{:href => "adjoint+functor#InTermsOfHomIsomorphism"} means
            that for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b,c \in C_0
            we have a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+bijection"} natural bijection
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≃
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Hom_C(a \otimes b, c)
                  \\;\simeq\;
                  Hom_C(a, [b,c])
                  \\,,
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural
            in all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variables"} arguments
            (see (
            %a{:href => "#MacLane97"}> MacLane (1971), §IV.7, theorem 3
            )).
          %p
            The object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [b,c]
            is called the
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hom(b,c)
            (and then sometimes underlined).
          %h3#cartesian_closed_monoidal_category Cartesian closed monoidal category
          %p
            If the monoidal structure of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian
            (and so in particular
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed"} cartesian closed
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+object"} exponential object
            and written
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi c
                      %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c^b
          %h3#left_right_and_biclosed_monoidal_category Left-, right- and bi-closed monoidal category
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal
            but not necessarily
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric
            or even
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+category"} braided
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -\otimes b
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b\otimes -
            may be inequivalent functors, and either one or both may have an adjoint. The terminology here is less standard, but many people use
            = succeed "," do
              %strong left closed
            = succeed "," do
              %strong right closed
            %strong biclosed
            monoidal category to indicate, respectively, that the left tensor product, the right tensor product functors or both have
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoints"} right adjoints
            %strong closed
            instead of biclosed.) So in particular a symmetric closed monoidal category is automatically biclosed.
          %p
            The analogue of exponential objects for monoidal categories are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/residual"} left and right residuals
          %h2#properties Properties
          #TensorHomIsoInternalizes.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{C}, \otimes, 1)
              a closed monoidal category with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
              denoted
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-,-]
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+bijections"} natural bijections
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Hom
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi Hom
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Hom_{\mathcal{C}}(X \otimes Y, Z)
                    \\simeq
                    Hom_{\mathcal{C}}(X, [Y,Z])
            %p
              but these isomorphisms themselves “internalize” to isomorphisms in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    [X \otimes Y, Z]
                    \\simeq
                    [X,[Y,Z]]
                    \\,.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              By the external natural bijections there is for every
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in \mathcal{C}
              a composite natural bijection
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Hom
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi Hom
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi Hom
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi Hom
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi Hom
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Hom_{\mathcal{C}}(A, [X \otimes Y, Z])
                    \\simeq
                    Hom_{\mathcal{C}}(A \otimes (X \otimes Y), Z)
                    \\simeq
                    Hom_{\mathcal{C}}((A \otimes X) \otimes Y, Z)
                    \\simeq
                    Hom_{\mathcal{C}}(A \otimes X, [Y,Z])
                    \\simeq
                    Hom_{\mathcal{C}}(A,[X,[Y,Z]])
                    \\,.
            %p
              Since this holds for every
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in \mathcal{C}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
              (namely the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+functor"} fully faithfulness
              of the
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    [X \otimes Y, Z] \simeq [X,[Y,Z]]
                    \\,.
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %p
          #ExampleSets.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              The tautological example is the category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
              of sets with its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
              (a
              = succeed "):" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+monoidal+category"} cartesian closed monoidal category
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+set"} function set
              %br/
          %p
          #ExamplePresheaves.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              In generalization of Exp.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ExampleSets"}
              and as a special case of Exp.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FunctorCategory"}
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+presheaves"} category of presheaves
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+category"} functor category
              of the form
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Func
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mi op
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Set
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Func(\mathcal{C}^{op}, Set)
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+monoidal+category"} cartesian closed monoidal category
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+structure+on+presheaves"} closed monoidal structure on presheaves
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              In further generalization of Exp
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ExamplePresheaves"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+monoidal+category"} cartesian closed monoidal category
          %p
          #AbelianGroups.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              The category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
              with its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+abelian+groups"} tensor product of abelian groups
              is (non-cartesian!) closed: for any two abelian groups
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A, B
              the set of homomorphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to B
              carries (pointwise defined) abelian group structure.
          %p
          #ExampleModules.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              In generalization of Exp.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AbelianGroups"}
              (which is the case
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R =
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℤ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mod"} Mod
              of
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
              with its usual
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+modules"} tensor product of modules
              is (non-cartesian!) closed monoidal. This is one of the first hom-tensor adjunctions that appeared in
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              In specialization of Exp.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ExampleModules"}
              to the case that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo =
                    %mi 𝕂
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R = \mathbb{K}
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝕂
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
              of
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝕂
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
              and
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝕂
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+maps"} linear maps
              between them is (non-cartesian!) closed monoidal with respect to the usual
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+vector+spaces"} tensor product of vector spaces
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
              assigns vector spaces of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+maps"} linear maps
              (with the vector operations given argument-wise):
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi W
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ∈
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi 𝕂
                    %mi Vect
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi V
                    %mo ,
                    %mi W
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mi 𝕂
                      %mi Vec
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo ,
                      %mi W
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "left", :displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi k
                            %mo ⋅
                            %mi f
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %mi v
                            %mo ↦
                            %mi k
                            %mo ⋅
                            %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi v
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi f
                            %mo +
                            %mi g
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %mi v
                            %mo ↦
                            %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi v
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo +
                            %mi g
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi v
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    V,\,W \,\in\, \mathbb{K}Vect
                    \\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\; \vdash \;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;
                    [V,W]
                    \\;\coloneqq\;
                    \\left(
                    \\mathbb{K}Vec(V,W)
                    ,\,
                    \\begin{array}{l}
                    k \cdot f \,\colon\, v \mapsto k \cdot f(v)
                    \\\
                    f + g \,\colon\, v \mapsto f(v) + g(v)
                    \\end{array}
                    \\right)
            %p
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+algebra"} higher homotopical
              variant of Exp.
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ExampleModules"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+chain+complexes"} category of chain complexes
              over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              is closed monoidal, via the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+chain+complexes"} tensor product of chain complexes
              and the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom+of+chain+complexes"} internal hom of chain complexes
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              Given a closed monoidal category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equalizers"} equalizers
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coequalizers"} coequalizers
              and a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monad"} commutative monad
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+a+monad"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                \-algebras
              inherits a closed monoidal structure. See at
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+algebras+over+a+commutative+monad"} tensor product of algebras over a commutative monad
          %p
          #FunctorCategory.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+category"} complete
              closed monoidal category and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              any
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+category"} functor category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi I
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [I,C]
              is closed monoidal with the pointwise tensor product,
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi F
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (F\otimes G)(x) = F(x) \otimes G(x)
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              Since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is complete, the category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi I
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [I,C]
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonadic+functor"} comonadic
              over
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mrow
                          %mi ob
                          %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{ob I}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+extension"} Kan extension
              along the inclusion
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ob
                      %mi I
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ob I \hookrightarrow I
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi I
                      %mo ,
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\in [I,C]
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi I
                            %mo ,
                            %mi C
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                                %mi F
                                %mo ⊗
                                %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi I
                            %mo ,
                            %mi C
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi C
                              %mrow
                                %mi ob
                                %mi I
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi F
                                  %mn 0
                                %mo ⊗
                                %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi C
                              %mrow
                                %mi ob
                                %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    [I,C]
                    \&amp; \overset{F\otimes - }{\longrightarrow} &amp;
                    [I,C]
                    \\\
                    \\big\downarrow &amp;&amp; \big\downarrow
                    \\\
                    C^{ob I}
                    \&amp; \underset{F_0 \otimes -}{\longrightarrow} &amp;
                    C^{ob I}
                    }
            %p
              This
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} commutes
              because the tensor product in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi I
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [I,C]
              is pointwise (here
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_0
              means the family of objects
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(x)
              in
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mrow
                          %mi ob
                          %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{ob I}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is closed,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_0 \otimes -
              has a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonadic+functor"} comonadic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+lifting+theorem"} adjoint lifting theorem
              implies that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\otimes -
              has a right adjoint as well.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+category"} discrete
              monoidal category (i.e., a
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semigroup"} semigroup
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}M
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monoid"} commutative monoid
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              is a closed monoidal (in fact,
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed"} compact closed
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi g
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo =
                      %mi f
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \otimes g = [f,g] = f g
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#EilenbergKelly65"} Eilenberg and Kelly (1965)
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Certain
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nice+category+of+spaces"} nice categories
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
                are cartesian closed: for any two nice enough topological spaces
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                the set of continuous maps
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to Y
                can be equipped with a topology to become a nice topological space itself.
            %li
              %p
                Certain nice categories of
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+object"} pointed/based
                topological spaces are closed symmetric monoidal. The monoidal structure is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smash+product"} smash product
                and the internal-hom is the set of basepoint-preserving maps with topology induced from the space of unbased ones.
            %li
              %p
                The category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
                is cartesian closed: the internal-hom is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+category"} functor category
                of functors and natural transformations. There is exactly one other closed monoidal structure on
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/funny+tensor+product"} funny tensor product
            %li
              %p
                The category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 Cat
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-category"} strict 2-categories
                and strict 2-functors is closed symmetric monoidal under the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gray+tensor+product"} Gray tensor product
                %em pseudo
                natural transformations, and modifications.
            %li
              %p
                The category of strict
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ω
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+omega-category"} categories
                is also biclosed monoidal, under the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Crans-Gray+tensor+product"} Crans-Gray tensor product
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                is a monoidal category and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Set
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi M
                            %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{M^{op}}
                is endowed with the tensor product given by the induced
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Day+convolution"} Day convolution
                product, then the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+presheaves"} category of presheaves
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Set
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi M
                            %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{M^{op}}
                is biclosed monoidal.
            %li
              %p
                The category of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/species"} species
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} - \circ G
                admits a right adjoint) but not biclosed monoidal.
            %li
              %p
                The category of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
                over any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+monoid"} Hopf monoid
                in a closed monoidal category, or more generally algebras for any
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+monad"} Hopf monad
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+object"} group object
                in a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed category
                is (cartesian) closed monoidal. For more on this phenomenon see at
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannaka+duality"} Tannaka duality
            %li
              %p
                The category of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+convex+topological+vector+spaces"} locally convex topological vector spaces
                with the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive+tensor+product"} inductive tensor product
                and internal hom the space of continuous linear maps with the topology of pointwise convergence is symmetric closed monoidal.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"}> monoidal category
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} monoidal (∞,1)-category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"}> symmetric monoidal category
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} symmetric monoidal (∞,1)-category
            %li
              %p
                %strong closed monoidal category
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} closed monoidal (∞,1)-category
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/doubly+closed+monoidal+category"} doubly closed monoidal category
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+functor"} closed monoidal functor
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indexed+closed+monoidal+category"} indexed closed monoidal category
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"}> cartesian closed category
                    ,
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+object"} exponential object
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"}> string diagram
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kelly-Mac+Lane+diagram"} Kelly-Mac Lane diagram
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linguistics"} natural language syntax
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li#EilenbergKelly65
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samuel+Eilenberg"}> Samuel Eilenberg
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G.+Max+Kelly"} G. Max Kelly
              = succeed "," do
                %em Closed Categories
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Proceedings+of+the+Conference+on+Categorical+Algebra+-+La+Jolla+1965"} Proceedings of the Conference on Categorical Algebra - La Jolla 1965
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-99902-4"} doi:10.1007/978-3-642-99902-4
          %p Textbook accounts:
          %ul
            %li#MacLane97
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+MacLane"}> Saunders MacLane
                , VII.7 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories+for+the+Working+Mathematician"} Categories for the Working Mathematician
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 5
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-1-4757-4721-8"} doi:10.1007/978-1-4757-4721-8
            %li#Borceux94
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"}> Francis Borceux
                , vol 2 section 6.1 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Categorical+Algebra"} Handbook of Categorical Algebra
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511525865"} doi:10.1017/CBO9780511525865
          %p Original articles studying monoidal biclosed categories are
          %ul
            %li#Lambek68
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joachim+Lambek"}> Joachim Lambek
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Deductive systems and categories
            %li#Lambek69
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joachim+Lambek"}> Joachim Lambek
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Deductive systems and categories II
          %p
            For more historical development see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "linear+type+theory#HistoryCategoricalSemantics"} linear type theory – History of linear categorical semantics
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched category theory
            the enriching category is taken to be closed monoidal. Accordingly the standard textbook on enriched category theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Max+Kelly"}> Max Kelly
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Basic concepts of enriched category theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 64
                = succeed ");" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.cambridge.org/de/academic/subjects/mathematics/logic-categories-and-sets/basic-concepts-enriched-category-theory?format=PB&isbn=9780521287029"} ISBN:9780521287029
              %p republished as:
              %p
                Reprints in Theory and Applications of Categories, No. 10 (2005) pp. 1-136 (
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/10/tr10abs.html"}> tac:tr10
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/10/tr10.pdf"} pdf
          %p has a chapter on just closed monoidal categories.
